    "date_label": "Origins of the New South (1951) · Reunion and Reaction (1951) · The Strange Career of Jim Crow (1955) · “The Future of the Past”: presidential address delivered (1969)",
    "description": "A historian of the American South whose work challenged accounts of an unchanging racial order. Bertram Wyatt-Brown’s substantive memorial presents The Strange Career of Jim Crow as an argument about the historical formation of segregation, while noting later revisions and declining scholarly influence. Woodward’s own presidential address argues that historians should expose anachronisms and mediate between aspirations and historical constraints. The entry keeps that civic role distinct from endorsing a consoling national past or treating a once influential interpretation as settled.",
    "representative_figures_and_works": "Origins of the New South (1951); Reunion and Reaction (1951; later editions with new prefaces); The Strange Career of Jim Crow (1955; repeatedly revised); “The Future of the Past,” presidential address (delivered 1969).",
    "source_ids": [
      "hc177_woodward_reader",
      "hc177_woodward_address",
      "hc177_woodward_reunion",
      "hc177_woodward_career"
    ],
    "scope_note": "New identity is Comer Vann Woodward, not another Woodward dissertation author. Named substantive reader supports the bounded book argument; own address supports civic-method claim. No King quotation, unchecked lecture date, whole-book reading or settled periodization claim. Book revisions and address delivery/publication remain distinct.",
